==English==

===Etymology===
{{PIE word|en|dwóh₁}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*lewk-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|twilight}}, {{m|enm|twyelyghte}}, equivalent to {{af|en|twi-|light|t1=double, half-}}, literally ‘second light, half-light’. Cognate to {{cog|sco|twa licht}}, {{m|sco|twylicht}}, {{m|sco|twielicht|t=twilight}}, {{cog|nds|twilecht}}, {{m|nds|twelecht|t=twilight}}, {{cog|nl|tweelicht|t=twilight, dusk}}, {{cog|de|Zwielicht|t=twilight, dusk}}. Compare {{cog|ang|twēone lēoht|t=twilight}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtwaɪˌlaɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-twilight.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The soft light in the sky seen before the rising and (especially) after the setting of the sun, occasioned by the illumination of the earth’s atmosphere by the direct rays of the sun and their reflection on the earth.
#: {{nearsyn|en|half-light}}
#: {{ux|en|I could just make out her face in the '''twilight'''.}}
# The time when this light is visible; the period between [[daylight]] and [[darkness]].
#: {{hypo|en|dawn|dusk}}
#: {{ux|en|It was '''twilight''' by the time I got back home.}}
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|II
|passage=At '''twilight''' in the summer there is never anybody to fear—man, woman, or cat—in the chambers and at that hour the mice come out. They do not eat parchment or foolscap or red tape, but they eat the luncheon crumbs.}}
# Any [[faint]] light through which something is seen.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1927|author=Willa Cather|title=s:en:Death Comes for the Archbishop|section=Book VII, section 4, page 233
|passage=Two women, Eusabio’s wife and sister, looked on from the deep '''twilight''' of the hut.}}
# {{lb|en|astronomy}} The time when the sun is less than 18° below the [[horizon]].
# {{lb|en|figurative|by extension}} An [[in-between]] or [[fading]] condition through which something is [[perceive]]d.
#: {{ux|en|The '''twilight''' of one's life}}
#* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding}}, ''Book IV, Chapter XIV''
#*: The '''twilight''' {{...}}of probability.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Dombey and Son|passage=“They have not enough heart,” said Edith, with a smile. The very '''twilight''' of a smile: so singularly were its light and darkness blended.}}

